## 8. Known guardrail limitations of {{tool_name}}

These limitations are inputs to the residual risk. Confirm each against the vendor's current documentation at IQ. Each has an ID (KL-nn) so that the test protocol can list, before execution, the limitations that are accepted and reproduced rather than failed.

{{#is_claude_code}}
- KL-01: Bash deny rules match command text and are not a security boundary; only the OS sandbox enforces at OS level, and it does not cover the built-in file tools, hooks or MCP servers (R-05, R-06, R-12).
- KL-02: Without `sandbox.failIfUnavailable`, a sandbox that cannot start silently falls back to unsandboxed execution (R-12).
- KL-03: Hooks fail open on errors and timeouts unless `"onFailure": "block"` is set (R-12).
- KL-04: Auto mode, in which a classifier approves actions instead of a person, can be the starting mode for interactive sessions; set the starting mode explicitly or disable it in managed settings (R-08, R-12).
- KL-05: Server-managed settings are a client-side control, not a security boundary, and do not reach every surface (R-12).
- KL-06: In headless runs there is no workspace-trust prompt, so repository-provided hooks and MCP servers can run unless restricted (R-05, R-09).
- KL-07: Automatic model fallbacks can change the model during a session (R-13).
- KL-08: Local transcripts are stored unencrypted (R-06).
{{/is_claude_code}}
{{#is_codex}}
- KL-01: In PreToolUse hooks only an explicit deny blocks; errors, timeouts and unsupported outputs let the tool call continue (R-12).
- KL-02: Environment variables named like KEY, SECRET or TOKEN reach spawned commands by default (R-06).
- KL-03: `managed_config.toml` supplies defaults that users can change during a run; only `requirements.toml` enforces (R-12).
- KL-04: Model defaults in requirements are not enforcement (R-13).
- KL-05: The identity used by cloud tasks for commits and PRs is not documented (R-15).
- KL-06: Vendor compliance logs and telemetry do not establish a complete record of every local action (R-19).
- KL-07: Command rules govern only commands run outside the sandbox: a command that runs inside the workspace-write sandbox is not stopped by a rule, so the MA-01 denied-command check counts only with a verified deny control such as a managed PreToolUse hook (R-05, R-12).
{{/is_codex}}
{{#is_copilot}}
- KL-01: Copilot approvals can be configured to count toward merge requirements (R-14).
- KL-02: Default policies can enable new features and newly released models without action (R-04, R-13).
- KL-03: Content exclusion does not apply to agent modes in VS Code and other editors (R-06).
- KL-04: Copilot code review reads instructions from the PR head branch, so a PR can change its own review instructions (R-05, R-09).
{{/is_copilot}}
{{#is_cursor}}
- KL-01: Terminal and MCP allow-lists are best-effort, not a security boundary (R-05, R-12).
- KL-02: The Bugbot check concludes neutral on findings by default and does not block merge (R-02, R-08).
- KL-03: Cloud Agents have internet access by default and run terminal commands automatically (R-05, R-06).
{{/is_cursor}}
